Let me also check for the CSS in the responsive section — there's a duplicate `.content-section` declaration. The first one is inside the `@media (max-width: 767px)` block at line ~1577, setting `padding: 40px 0`, and then another one outside at line ~1587, setting `padding: 60px 0`. The second one will override the first because it comes later in the CSS cascade. But the mobile one is inside a media query, so it should still take precedence on mobile since it's more specific. Wait, actually — the order matters in CSS. Let me check:

The CSS flow (roughly):
1. Line 1544-1579: Old mobile media query
2. Line 1581: Comment `/* ─── Structured content sections ───────────────────────── */`
3. Line 1583: `.content-section { padding: 60px 0; }` — This is outside the media query

The mobile version inside the media query:
```css
@media (max-width: 767px) {
  ...
  .content-section { padding: 40px 0; }
}
```

And later outside:
```css
.content-section { padding: 60px 0; }
```

The outside one will override the mobile one on all screen sizes because it comes later in the cascade. This is a bug — on mobile, the padding should be 40px, but it will be 60px. The mobile rule needs to come AFTER the base rule, or the media query needs to be more specific.

Actually wait, CSS precedence: both have the same specificity (single class selector). The second `.content-section { padding: 60px 0; }` at line 1583 will override the first one at line 1577 (inside the media query) — even on mobile — because it comes later in the stylesheet. The media query doesn't add specificity.

